      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bonham dates to 1837 when Bailey Inglish built Fort Inglish, a two‑story blockhouse that served as the town’s first refuge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonham%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was renamed Bonham in 1844 to honor James Butler Bonham, a defender of the Alamo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonham%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1866 the Bonham News, the county’s first newspaper, began publishing, marking the start of local journalism.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/bonham-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1888 Bonham boasted 117 businesses, three colleges, and a furniture factory, reflecting rapid growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/bonham-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Sam Rayburn House and Library, home to the former Speaker of the House, are open to the public in Bonham.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
